    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire.jpg" alt="Byzantine Empire" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Roman_Empire_565_CE.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mnohohrishnyi, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Khazar Khaganate emerged in the mid-seventh century on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic-Caspian steppe</a> following the fragmentation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a>. It was established by Turkic-speaking nomadic elites who consolidated power across territories stretching from the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> to the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and the steppes north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, subjugating or displacing rivals such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Kubrat</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a>. The ruling dynasty, probably a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashina-clan">Ashina clan</a> of the earlier Turkic khaganates, supplied the ideological framework for legitimacy. The Khazars capitalised on the vacuum left by earlier steppe confederations and on the rivalry between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and the Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, positioning themselves as intermediary and buffer between the two. By the late seventh century they controlled key trade corridors linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dual-kingship">dual kingship</a>, with a sacral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> and an active military ruler, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bek">bek</a>, reflected both inherited steppe traditions and pragmatic governance.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/atil"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atil.jpg" alt="Atil" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/atil">Atil</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Varangian_routes.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Briangotts, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Khazar economy combined nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">pastoralism</a> with sedentary agriculture and commerce. Much of the elite and population maintained steppe livelihoods centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-breeding">horse breeding</a>, livestock herding and seasonal migration, wintering in towns and summering on the pastures. The Khazars also controlled urban centres such as the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atil">Atil</a> on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the fortress-town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarkel">Sarkel</a> on the Don, which served as hubs of trade and administration. Their economy was closely tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-trade-route">Volga trade route</a>, which connected the Islamic world with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-europe">Northern Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">Furs</a>, wax, honey, slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> passed through Khazar territory, and the state levied a tithe on goods moving along the rivers and caravan roads. It also drew tribute from subordinate Slavic, Finnic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> peoples. A distinctive feature of Khazar society was its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-pluralism">religious pluralism</a>, encompassing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tengrism">Tengrist</a> beliefs, which eased commercial dealings with diverse partners.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/arab-khazar-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arab-khazar-wars.jpg" alt="Arab–Khazar wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/arab-khazar-wars">Arab–Khazar wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Caucasus,_740_CE.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Constantine Plakidas, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The military strength of the Khazar Khaganate was rooted in highly mobile cavalry, embodying the classic characteristics of steppe warfare. Khazar armies consisted primarily of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> capable of rapid movement, co-ordinated assaults and feigned withdrawals, allowing them to dominate vast steppe regions, while the rulers also maintained a standing guard of Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsiyah">Arsiyah</a>. Organisation drew on Turkic traditions, with units structured around tribal affiliations and elite retinues. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-khazar-wars">Arab–Khazar wars</a> of the seventh and eighth centuries the Khazars fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, destroying an Arab army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marj-ardabil">Marj Ardabil</a> in 730; although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marwan-ii">Marwan ibn Muhammad</a>'s great campaign of 737 forced a temporary submission, they ultimately held the line of the Caucasus against Islamic expansion. Fortifications such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarkel">Sarkel</a>, built with Byzantine engineers around 840, reflected an increasing emphasis on defence. The Khazars controlled tributary peoples through coercion and alliance, but their reliance on mobility and decentralised power limited their capacity to withstand sustained assaults from emerging regional powers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Governance in the Khazar Khaganate was distinguished by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dual-kingship">dual kingship</a>, which separated sacral and executive authority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> served as a secluded, sacral figurehead embodying divine favour, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bek">bek</a> exercised practical political and military power. This arrangement provided both symbolic continuity and functional government. Administrative control extended over a diverse population of Turkic, Slavic, Finnic and other peoples, managed through direct rule in core regions and indirect control over tributaries. Justice was pluralistic, reflecting the realm's religious diversity: Arab writers describe separate judges at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atil">Atil</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Christians, Muslims and pagans. The ruling elite's conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, traditionally dated to the eighth or ninth century, is often interpreted as a means of preserving independence from both Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and the Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>. Bureaucratic structures were limited compared with those of sedentary empires but effective in managing trade and tribute, and Atil functioned as the principal administrative and judicial centre.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/volga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/volga.jpg" alt="Volga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yaroslavl._Volga_River._Cathedral_of_the_Dormition_P5212700_2200.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexxx1979, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Khazar Khaganate played a pivotal role in shaping the political and economic dynamics of early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>. As a buffer between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, it helped halt Islamic expansion north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, influencing the religious and political trajectory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>. Its ties with Byzantium were close: Constantine V married the Khazar princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzitzak">Tzitzak</a>, and their son reigned as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-iv-the-khazar">Leo IV</a>, known as "the Khazar". Its control over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> route facilitated the exchange of goods, coinage and cultural practices between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, the Slavic world and the Islamic world, helping to channel the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> that became a cornerstone of Viking-age commerce in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-pluralism">religious pluralism</a> and the adoption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> created a unique cultural identity that continues to attract scholarly attention. Regionally, Khazar dominance over steppe and river networks shaped the development of emerging states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a>, whose early rulers even adopted the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>, and Khazar influence persisted in the political structures and trade patterns of successor polities.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev.jpg" alt="Sviatoslav I of Kiev" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I of Kiev</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sviatoslav_by_Eugene_Lanceray_1886.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yevgeny Lansere, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The decline of the Khazar Khaganate unfolded over the tenth century, driven by a combination of internal weakening and external pressure. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz</a> moved into the western steppes, and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a> challenged Khazar dominance over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> trade routes. In the 960s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslavs-campaign-against-the-khazars">Sviatoslav's campaign against the Khazars</a>, waged by Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I of Kiev</a> probably with Oghuz allies, culminated in the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarkel">Sarkel</a> around 965 and the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atil">Atil</a>, effectively dismantling the khaganate's political infrastructure. Economic decline accompanied these defeats, as control of lucrative trade routes shifted to rival powers. Internal factors, including the loosening of control over tributary peoples and possible divisions within the elite, further weakened the state, and changes in steppe ecology may also have contributed. By the late tenth century the Khazar Khaganate had ceased to exist as a centralised polity; scattered Khazar remnants survived for a time, but the region was absorbed into the expanding influence of neighbouring powers.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Khazar Khaganate's significance lies in its role as the steppe power between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and the Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a>, whose control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-trade-route">Volga trade route</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dual-kingship">dual kingship</a>, and rare <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-pluralism">religious pluralism</a>, including its ruling elite's conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, shaped early medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a> brought it down in the tenth century.</em></p>

